# test/capdisclose.map -- CAP-DISCLOSE-LINT (B43): how each answer-path cap constant discloses that it fired.
# Read by test/lib/capdisclose.py (its docstring is the contract); gated by test/capdisclosemapcheck.sh.
# Columns (TAB-separated): kName  CLASS  [awaits:#N]
#   sink:a,b        every function with a cap site of kName mentions a or b -- removing that disclosure reds the gate
#   legend          a static_assert ties kName to the legend sentence that states the cap
#   none:<reason>   not an answer cut
#   pending:<id> r  a cut whose function names no disclosure identifier: baselined, printed as INFO, to audit
#   awaits:#N       the constant is on an unmerged lane (absent = INFO, present = checked)
# How the rows were made (2026-11-12): `capdisclose.py . /dev/null --draft` proposed every row from the identifiers present
# at EVERY cap site's function (capped / more / cut / shown / floor / truncated / DISCLOSE ...). A sink row therefore holds
# the disclosure that is there today in place; it does not prove that identifier reports THIS cap -- a reviewer who reads a
# function and finds otherwise reclassifies the row to pending. #614's depth cap and #613's reader cap are the two rows read
# by hand (CodeRabbit 4542271958 and 4540061421): both lanes' red revisions FAIL their row and the fixed revisions PASS.
# A new cap constant on the answer path needs a row here in the same commit.
